Subject: ictrobot_shm_v1: add the shared-memory imports

WASIX has no cross-process shared memory: wasix-libc implements mmap
inside the guest, fork deep-copies linear memory, and a MAP_SHARED
mapping becomes an independent copy in every virtual process. A
multi-process guest built around a shared segment cannot run
correctly, and no guest-side change can fix that.

Add an explicit, opt-in ictrobot_shm_v1 import namespace with fd_map
and fd_unmap: native shared file overlays over page-aligned regions of
the calling instance's linear memory, backed by a memfd-based
/dev/shm. Forked children re-overlay their inherited mappings; as on
Linux, exec keeps none: a recycled execution unregisters its ranges
and a new image re-attaches explicitly. Futexes on mapped addresses
take their identity from the shared object and offset, so waits and
wakes pair across instances. A module that imports the namespace
fails loudly to instantiate anywhere the imports are absent.

The mapping list lives beside the process, outside its inner lock,
and the futex path checks an atomic count before taking any lock, so
a process that never maps pays one relaxed load per wait or wake and
never queues behind thread or signal bookkeeping.

The namespace is registered for the 32-bit WASI versions only: there
is no wasm64 sysroot to build a guest with, so 64-bit entry points
would be untestable declarations. A future 64-bit ABI can arrive as
its own namespace once a guest can exist to import it.

